WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.079 --> 00:00:08.562
On May 26, 1940, the German army cornered British and French soldiers on the beaches of Dunkirk, France.

2
00:00:08.564 --> 00:00:22.728
Over the next ten days, about 338,000 desperate soldiers were rescued and evacuated across the English Channel to Britain by hundreds of civilian and military boats, to escape the German army.

3
00:00:22.729 --> 00:00:33.236
Winston Churchill had only been prime minister of England for about a month when he spoke to the House of Commons on June 4, 1940, after the retreat at Dunkirk:

4
00:00:33.237 --> 00:00:40.323
"We shall defend our island, whatever the cost may be, we shall fight on the beaches . . .

5
00:00:40.324 --> 00:00:48.356
We shall fight in the fields and in the streets, we shall fight in the hills; we shall never surrender."

6
00:00:48.357 --> 00:00:58.497
Through the years of hardship to come during World War II, the British people would turn to Churchill and his inspiring words again and again.

7
00:00:58.498 --> 00:01:08.444
Churchill was born in England on November 30, 1874, to Lord Randolph Churchill and Jennie Jerome, an American heiress.

8
00:01:08.444 --> 00:01:16.913
Four weeks before his eighth birthday, Churchill was sent to a boarding school and remained away from home for most of his childhood.

9
00:01:16.914 --> 00:01:24.883
He missed his parents very much and would write letters to his mother and father begging them to visit. They rarely did.

10
00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:30.322
Once, he read in the newspaper that his father had given a speech nearby.

11
00:01:30.322 --> 00:01:39.448
He wrote, "I cannot think why you did not come to see me . . . I was very disappointed, but I suppose you were too busy to come."

12
00:01:39.448 --> 00:01:48.386
At age 14, he was sent to Harrow, an elite public school, and it was there that his remarkable gift for language blossomed.

13
00:01:48.387 --> 00:02:00.989
Despite a speech impediment, a lisp that made it hard for him to speak clearly, he won a school competition by reciting over 1,200 lines of poetry from memory.

14
00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:10.029
But Lord Randolph did not think his son had the intelligence to go into politics, so Churchill was encouraged to become a soldier. In

15
00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:20.217
1893, Churchill entered Sandhurst, the British military college; a year later, he graduated and joined the army. As

16
00:02:20.218 --> 00:02:32.421
a cavalry officer between 1895 and 1898, Churchill fought in Cuba, India, what is now northwestern Pakistan, and Sudan. He

17
00:02:32.422 --> 00:02:44.211
wrote about his military experiences, and after leaving the military in 1899, he wrote news reports from places as far flung as Egypt and South Africa. In

18
00:02:44.212 --> 00:02:49.704
South Africa, he was taken prisoner, escaping with a bounty on his head. His

19
00:02:49.706 --> 00:02:59.166
thrilling tale of capture and escape, London to Ladysmith via Pretoria , made him a popular, well-paid author.

20
00:02:59.167 --> 00:03:10.160
In 1900, he won a seat in Parliament and went on a speaking tour in the United States, where his speeches revealed a powerful oratory style.

21
00:03:10.161 --> 00:03:20.903
He met the famous American writer Mark Twain, who introduced the 26-year-old Churchill to a New York audience as "the future prime minister of England."

22
00:03:20.904 --> 00:03:35.755
In 1911, Churchill was named first lord of the Admiralty, and when World War I broke out in 1914, he found himself desperately looking for a way around the trench warfare stalemate on the western front.

23
00:03:35.756 --> 00:03:45.022
Churchill decided to get around the impasse by attacking the Turks, who were allied with Germany, at Constantinople (now Istanbul).

24
00:03:45.023 --> 00:03:53.007
Churchill sent soldiers to the Gallipoli Peninsula, on the coast of Turkey, but there was fierce resistance from the Turks.

25
00:03:53.008 --> 00:03:59.383
When the British soldiers landed on the beaches, the enemy had the high ground and smashed them.

26
00:03:59.384 --> 00:04:06.782
More than 250,000 troops died at Gallipoli, and Churchill’s strategy was widely blamed.

27
00:04:06.783 --> 00:04:11.338
"I thought he would never get over it," his wife, Clementine, said.

28
00:04:11.338 --> 00:04:20.128
"I thought he would die of grief." Churchill resigned his position, but by 1917, he was back in government.

29
00:04:20.129 --> 00:04:34.324
Over the years, Churchill held different governmental posts, but he left again in 1931 to devote himself completely to finishing The World Crisis , a six-volume history of the First World

30
00:04:34.325 --> 00:04:48.426
War. In the early 1930s, while visiting famous World War I European battlefields, Churchill noticed uniformed youth marching in the streets and became convinced that Hitler was planning

31
00:04:48.427 --> 00:05:00.695
war. He began warning about the dangers posed by Nazi Germany and pointed out England’s lack of military readiness, but others did not share his views, and he was largely

32
00:05:00.695 --> 00:05:09.437
ignored. Then, in 1938, when Germany invaded Czechoslovakia, some politicians turned to Churchill for

33
00:05:09.437 --> 00:05:18.078
guidance. But Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain still preferred to negotiate with the German dictator rather than resist

34
00:05:18.078 --> 00:05:26.820
him. Germany invaded Poland in 1939, and Britain and France declared war on Germany—World War II had

35
00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:36.836
begun. In the spring of 1940, George VI, the king of England, lost confidence in Chamberlain as prime minister and replaced him with

36
00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:46.664
Churchill. The task ahead was daunting: England and France were fighting together against Germany, but Churchill knew they were not powerful enough to defeat

37
00:05:46.664 --> 00:05:58.890
Hitler. During the war years, Churchill gave the greatest speeches of his life, inspiring the British people to stand strong against Nazi Germany and urging other nations to join the

38
00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:03.617
battle. "Hitler knows that he will have to break us . . . or lose the

39
00:06:03.617 --> 00:06:14.398
war. If we can stand up to him, all Europe may be free and the life of the world may move forward into broad, sunlit uplands," he said in one stirring

40
00:06:14.398 --> 00:06:28.758
speech. Visiting Harrow, Churchill spoke to the students. "This is the lesson," he said. "Never give in, never give in, never, never,

41
00:06:28.758 --> 00:06:34.898
never." In the summer of 1940, the Battle of Britain was being fought in the skies over

42
00:06:34.898 --> 00:06:43.609
England. Hitler’s bombs fell day and night. It was a terrible time for England, but British fighter pilots fought bravely.

43
00:06:43.609 --> 00:06:53.984
"Never in the field of human conflict was so much owed by so many to so few," Churchill said of the tireless Royal Air Force.

44
00:06:53.984 --> 00:07:04.570
By 1941, Churchill was working to forge a "grand alliance" with America and the Soviet Union, countries with vast military resources.

45
00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:13.406
Later, he said, "If we are together, nothing is impossible. If we are divided, all will fail."

46
00:07:13.406 --> 00:07:22.785
By 1945, Churchill’s grand alliance had triumphed, and on May 8, victory in Europe had been declared.

47
00:07:22.785 --> 00:07:35.262
The end of the war brought political turmoil as England faced the task of rebuilding, and just a few months later, shockingly, Churchill was defeated in a general election.

48
00:07:35.262 --> 00:07:44.504
Although he was out of office, Churchill was respected around the globe as a legendary speaker, statesman, and author.

49
00:07:44.504 --> 00:08:03.727
His books The Second World War and A History of the English-Speaking Peoples met with worldwide acclaim, and in 1953, Churchill won the Nobel Prize in Literature in part for The Second World War and his many inspiring speeches.

50
00:08:03.727 --> 00:08:14.845
In 1951, Churchill was again prime minister, but resigned in 1955 at the age of 81 due to weariness and poor health.

51
00:08:14.845 --> 00:08:21.837
He died on January 24, 1965, at the age of ninety.

52
00:08:21.837 --> 00:08:30.884
In 2002, the British Broadcasting Corporation asked the people of England to vote on who was the greatest Briton.

53
00:08:30.884 --> 00:08:37.376
William Shakespeare, Princess Diana, and Charles Darwin were among the candidates.

54
00:08:37.376 --> 00:08:47.126
Nearly fifty years after his death, Churchill won over a million votes and was named the greatest Briton of all time.

55
00:08:47.126 --> 00:08:53.673
Churchill’s brilliant speeches live on today and are often quoted by world leaders.

56
00:08:53.673 --> 00:09:09.329
"I have nothing to offer but blood, toil, tears, and sweat," he had said in his first speech as prime minister, but it was exactly what England, and perhaps the entire world, needed at the time.

